  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "An ocean liner sits rotting in the middle of a desert. Not a museum piece, but a ghost relic left behind in the cracked alkali dirt of Southern California. How did deep-water wreckage end up miles from the nearest wave?",
        "visual_query": "abandoned boat desert landscape",
        "visual_fallback": "dry desert landscape"
      },
      {
        "narration": "In 1905, an engineering mistake diverted the full, rushing force of the Colorado River straight into a dry desert basin. For two years, millions of gallons poured unchecked into the sinkhole, creating a massive inland sea where nothing had lived for centuries.",
        "visual_query": "colorado river canyon landscape",
        "visual_fallback": "rushing river water"
      },
      {
        "narration": "By the 1950s, elite Hollywood stars flocked to its glittering shores. Yacht clubs buzzed, luxury hotels sold out, and developers marketed the accidental oasis as the new Riviera. But what happens when paradise is built on a landlocked trap?",
        "visual_query": "vintage 1950s resort pool",
        "visual_fallback": "sunny beach resort"
      },
      {
        "narration": "With no outlet to the ocean, the water had nowhere to go. Agricultural runoff poisoned the basin, triggering massive fish die-offs and an unbearable smell of decay that chased away every tourist overnight.",
        "visual_query": "dead fish salt shore",
        "visual_fallback": "barren shoreline"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The modern disaster wasn't a curse, but a math problem: a failed irrigation canal built by the California Development Company trapped water with zero escape routes. Can a dying desert ecosystem ever be engineered back to life?",
        "visual_query": "aerial view salton sea",
        "visual_fallback": "desert aerial view"
      }
    ],
